.TH QFW-DIR-SVC 1 "August 2026" "QFw 0.1.0" "QFw Commands"
.SH NAME
qfw-dir-svc \- manage one QFw directory-service instance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qfw-dir-svc
.I start|run
.B --run-dir
.I path
[
.I options
]
.PP
.B qfw-dir-svc
.I status|stop
.B --run-dir
.I path
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B qfw-dir-svc
starts, supervises, inspects, or stops a directory service without owning a
QPM. After a live start it atomically publishes the resolved name and endpoint
to the
.B directory-service.connection-file
configured in
.BR qfw-site.yaml (5).
.PP
In application scope the directory service runs on the head of allocation
group 1. In site scope
.B --node
selects its node. Remote placement requires a Slurm allocation; local mode can
launch only on the calling node.
.PP
.BR qfw-setup (1)
invokes this lifecycle engine automatically for runtime profiles that request
an application-owned directory. Direct application-scope invocation is mainly
an integration and diagnostic interface.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B start
Start in the background and return after readiness.
.TP
.B run
Remain in the foreground for a service supervisor. SIGINT and SIGTERM stop the
owned directory process.
.TP
.B status
Probe the recorded process and print generated state as JSON.
.TP
.B stop
Stop the owned directory process and update generated state.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --run-dir " path"
Private lifecycle state, PID, readiness, and log directory.
.TP
.BI --site-config " path"
Site configuration. The default comes from the activated QFw environment.
.TP
.BI --runtime-config " path"
Runtime configuration used to resolve application allocation policy.
.TP
.BI --scope " application|site"
Select application-owned or site-owned lifetime and placement rules.
.TP
.BI --node " node"
Site-scope launch node. This option is rejected in application scope.
.TP
.BI --timeout " seconds"
Startup timeout. The default is 120 seconds.
.TP
.BI --poll-interval " seconds"
Foreground supervision interval used by
.BR run .
The default is 2 seconds.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Resolve and record the plan without starting a process or publishing the
client connection record.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates successful startup, ready status, or shutdown. Status returns
nonzero when the instance is not ready. Configuration, readiness, and cleanup
errors also return nonzero.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qfw-qpm-svc (1),
.BR qfw-setup (1),
.BR qfw-status (1),
.BR qfw-site.yaml (5),
.BR qfw-service-lifecycle (7)
